# DESCRIPTION

sky1-reset owns the registers of one of the Sky1's two reset controllers, and holds or releases the reset lines in them for the devices whose `RSTL` names them.
`RST0`, the S5 system controller (`CIXHA020`), is at `0x16000000`, and holds 143 lines: the DDR controller, the interrupt controller, the interconnects, the GPU, the NPU and the video unit, the image signal processor, the display engines and their transmitters, audio, the camera interfaces, the Ethernet MACs, PCIe, the USB PHYs and controllers, and each subsystem's RCSU.
`RST1`, the FCH controller (`CIXHA021`), is at `0x04160000`, and holds 29: the I3C, UART, SPI and I2C blocks' functional and APB resets, the GPT timer, the DMA, GPIO and the XSPI controller.
devmgr(8) starts one for each, before the devices that name it:

```text
driver sky1-reset
class clock
match acpi CIXHA020
match acpi CIXHA021
```

Every line is one bit of a register word, and is active low: the bit clear holds its block in reset, the bit set releases it.
A line's index is the one a device's `RSTL` gives, the same on both firmware and a devicetree.
Firmware leaves the controllers live, with other devices' resets released in the same words, so a change reads the line's word and writes it back with that bit alone changed, one command at a time, and writes nothing where the bit already reads as asked.
No bit is written that no command names.

A device that needs a reset reads its `RSTL` in its `/dev/acpi/links`, a line `reset <name> <service> <index>`, and writes the commands below to `/dev/provider/<service>/ctl`, which devmgr(8) mounts in its driver because the link names the controller.
No other driver maps either controller's page.
A device's driver that turns on its links (the way k1-syscon(3)'s consumers do) writes `reset <index> deassert`.
mali(3) and linlon(3) pulse the GPU's and a display engine's resets this way.

# FILES

The tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at the controller's directory under `drivers`, and at `/dev/provider/<service>` in the drivers of the devices that link to it.

: `ctl`
  Takes a command a line: `reset <index> assert`, `reset <index> deassert`, or `reset <index> pulse <us>`, the index in decimal or `0x` hexadecimal.
  A pulse asserts the line, waits the microseconds, releases it, and waits as long again before it answers, since a block needs time to come out of reset; the hold is at most 100000.
  A line it can't parse, a pulse longer than that, or an index the controller doesn't have fails the write as invalid, and the driver logs which.
  A write that holds the driver's one lock for a pulse delays any other command to the same controller until it is done.
: `status`
  Read only.
  The controller, `s5: CIXHA020, 143 resets` (or `fch: CIXHA021, 29 resets`); a line `reset <index> asserted` or `deasserted` for each line a command has named, as its bit reads now; `commands <n>, failed <n>`; and `last failure: <command>: <why>`.

